      "narrative": "Mara Venn's eyebrow lifts a fraction \u2014 not at the question, but at how quickly you've zeroed in on the same thread she's been pulling.\n\n\"Toran Vex,\" she says, tilting her head toward the bar. \"Him. The man with ink up to his elbows.\"\n\nShe lowers her voice, the scrape of a chair against floorboards covering her words.\n\n\"He's the Kestrel Guild's lockwright. Has been for seven years. Every quarter-moon, he'd walk up to the Ash Gate with his oil can and his ring of brass tools, and he'd spend an hour tuning the mechanism. The Guild trusted him with that Gate like a priest trusts a sexton with the altar.\"\n\nShe pauses, letting a serving girl pass before continuing.\n\n\"But here's the detail that keeps me up at night.\" She counts on her fingers. \"Three days ago, Bram dies. The Gate is sealed. And Toran Vex \u2014 the only man alive who knows every pin and lever of that lock \u2014 has been sitting in this tavern for three solid days, ordering watered ale and staring at the same page of his ledger without turning it.\"\n\nShe lets that land.\n\n\"He hasn't gone near the Gate since Bram's body was carried past this window. Not once. Not to check the lock. Not to see if it was tampered with. Not to offer the Guild his expertise.\"\n\nMara's eyes glint in the copper light.\n\n\"A man who spent seven years tending a lock like a garden doesn't just stop. Unless he already knows what happened to it. Or unless he's afraid of what he'd find.\"\n\nShe picks up her wine, takes a sip, and watches you over the rim.\n\n\"So. You want to know who maintained the lock? Toran Vex. And he's sitting twenty feet away, pretending he can't hear us.\""
    },
