      "narrative": "The copper lamps dim behind you as the tavern door swings shut, cutting off the low murmur of conversation. Morning air hits you \u2014 damp, carrying the mineral tang of rain-soaked stone and something older, like river silt left to dry in the sun.\n\nThe street curves past shuttered storefronts, their windows dark. A single figure hurries across an intersecting alley, head down, boots splashing in a puddle. They don't look at you.\n\nAnd then you see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises at the end of the lane where the cobbles give way to packed earth. It's not a gate in the way of wood and hinges \u2014 it's a cracked black arch, twice the height of a man, standing alone against the grey sky. No wall flanks it. No building supports it. Just the arch, dark stone veined with pale streaks like ash that never washed away, framing a view of the river beyond.\n\nBut the river is wrong. Through the arch, the water lies still and glassy, reflecting clouds that don't match the ones above you. The Grey Hind \u2014 a long, narrow boat \u2014 is moored to a stone cleat on the other side, its hull dark and empty.\n\nA heavy iron gate has been lowered across the arch's opening. Its bars are thick as a child's wrist, and a padlock the size of a fist secures the chain that holds it shut. The lock's keyhole is an odd shape \u2014 not a simple cross or star, but something with fine internal ridges, like the guts of a clock.\n\nThe ground at the base of the arch is scuffed. Fresh scuffs. Boot marks in the damp earth, heading toward the gate and stopping. Someone's been here recently.\n\nThe air around the arch is cold. Colder than the morning around it."
    },
