      "narrative": "The name leaves your throat and hangs in the damp air \u2014 \"Old Bram!\" \u2014 carrying past the iron bars, through the arch, across the still water beyond.\n\nThe echo bounces off the stone, once, twice, then fades into nothing.\n\nNo voice answers from the Grey Hind. No creak of footsteps on the boat's deck. No shuffle of movement from the shadows beneath the arch.\n\nThe river on the other side doesn't ripple. The clouds reflected in its surface don't shift. The whole scene beyond the gate has the stillness of a painted backdrop \u2014 too quiet, too patient, as if the world on the other side is holding its breath.\n\nA breeze curls through the bars, carrying the faint smell of damp stone and iron rust. Nothing else.\n\nThe silence stretches.\n\nAnd in that silence, your eyes catch something you didn't notice before \u2014 a detail half-hidden in the shadow of the arch's base. A scrap of dark fabric, snagged on a sharp edge of stone where the gate's frame meets the ground. It's small, no bigger than your palm, and it's been there long enough for the morning dew to bead along its edge.\n\nThe boots scuffs lead toward the gate and stop. But this scrap \u2014 it's caught on the *inside* of the arch's frame, past where the gate would seal shut."
    },
