      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch, running your fingers through the damp earth beneath the arch, searching for anything the morning light might have missed. The soil is cool and packed \u2014 you sift through it, palm flat, eyes scanning for disturbed patches, for hidden compartments, for anything that might explain the scuffs or the fabric.\n\nNothing.\n\nThe dirt is uniform. No loose stones hiding a key. No faint outline of a buried object. No change in texture where someone might have dug and refilled. Just wet earth, root-fragments, and the small pebbles that belong here.\n\nYou sit back on your heels, fingers dark with soil, and the silence from the other side of the gate feels heavier now \u2014 like it's watching you fail to find what's right in front of you.\n\nThe fabric scrap still hangs from the stone inside the arch. The boot scuffs still lead to the gate and stop. The padlock still sits, fat and unyielding, its clockwork keyhole catching the grey light.\n\nNothing beneath the earth offers you an answer."
