      "narrative": "The walk back is shorter than it should be \u2014 or maybe the morning just has a way of collapsing distance when your mind is full of iron gates and clockwork locks and fabric caught on stone.\n\nThe Copper Finch's door groans open under your hand. The air inside is warm, thick with the smell of old ale, woodsmoke, and the faint tang of the rain that still clings to your cloak. The copper lamps are lit now, casting a low amber glow across the scarred tables.\n\nYour table is empty. The one by the window, where the light falls at an angle across the grain of the wood. A half-melted candle stub sits in a tin holder, and someone's left a damp ring from a cup that's already been cleared away.\n\nA few heads turn as you enter \u2014 the usual morning crowd, thin and quiet. A man in a patched coat nurses something dark at the bar. Two women talk in low voices near the hearth, their words swallowed by the crackle of fresh logs.\n\nBehind the bar, a figure moves. Not Mara Venn \u2014 a younger woman with sleeves rolled to her elbows, wiping a cup with a rag. She glances up as you sit, then returns to her work without a word.\n\nThe table creaks as you settle into your seat. The window shows you the street outside, grey and damp, and beyond it, the direction of the Ash Gate."
